thods to injury prevention</article-title><source>Epidemiol Rev</source><year>2003</year><volume>25</volume><fpage>65</fpage><lpage>76</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12923991</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib14"><label>14.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Trifiletti</surname><given-names>LB</given-names></name><name><surname>Gielen</surname><given-names>AC</given-names></name><name><surname>Sleet</surname><given-names>DA</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Behavioral and social sciences theories and models: are they used in unintentional injury prevention research?</article-title><source>Health Educ Res</source><year>2005</year><volume>20</volume><fpage>298</fpage><lpage>307</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15632096</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib15"><label>15.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Bouter</surname><given-names>LM</given-names></name><name><surname>van Rijn</surname><given-names>OJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Kok</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Importance of planned health education for burn injury prevention</article-title><source>Burns</source><year>1990</year><volume>16</volume><fpage>198</fpage><lpage>202</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">2200425</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib16"><label>16.</label><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Rogers</surname><given-names>RW</given-names></name></person-group><person-group person-group-type="editor"><name><surname>Gochman</surname><given-names>DS</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Prentice-Dunn S. Protection motivation theory</article-title><source>Handbook of Health Behavior Research 1: Personal and Social Determinants</source><year>1997</year><publisher-loc>New York</publisher-loc><publisher-name>Plenum Press</publisher-name><fpage>113</fpage><lpage>32</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="bib17"><label>17.</label><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Rogers</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name></person-group><person-group person-group-type="editor"><name><surname>Cacioppo</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Petty</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Cognitive and physiological processes in fear-based attitude change: a revised theory of protection motivation</article-title><source>Social Psychophysiology: A Sourcebook</source><year>1983</year><publisher-loc>New York</publisher-loc><publisher-name>Guilford</publisher-name><fpage>153</fpage><lpage>76</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="bib18"><label>18.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Morrongiello</surname><given-names>BA</given-names></name><name><surname>Kiriakou</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Mothers&#x02019; home-safety practices for preventing six types of childhood injuries: what do they do, and why?</article-title><source>J Pediatr Psychol</source><year>2004</year><volume>29</volume><fpage>285</fpage><lpage>97</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15148351</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib19"><label>19.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Sellstrom</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name><name><surname>Bremberg</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Perceived social norms as crucial determinants of mother's injury-preventive behaviour</article-title><source>Acta Paediatr</source><year>1996</year><volume>85</volume><fpage>702</fpage><lpage>7</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8816208</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib20"><label>20.</label><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>